1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,580
 Hey everyone, Ryan here and welcome back to our Operative Dentistry series.

2
00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:13,000
 In this video, we'll cover everything from hand instruments to hand pieces and burs used

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:14,240
 in Operative Dentistry.

4
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:18,120
 So put on your seat belts and let's get to it.

5
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:25,520
 So the two main categories of dental hand instruments are non-cutting and cutting instruments.

6
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:29,760
 This is certainly not a list of every dental instrument out there, but the ones that are

7
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:33,600
 most commonly used, and that'll come up on the board exam.

8
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:38,600
 In the second half of the video, we'll talk about hand pieces, which fall into their

9
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,400
 own category.

10
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:47,000
 So dental hand instruments have three main regions.

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:55,040
 The handle, which is represented here by the letter C, is usually about 6 mm in diameter.

12
00:00:55,040 --> 00:01:01,120
 It can be eight-sided, like the one shown here, or it can be round, and sometimes it

13
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:06,080
 has a ribbed grip, like you see here, or it can be smooth and flat.

14
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:12,720
 The shank is represented here by the letter B, and the shank may have one or more bends

15
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:13,720
 in it.

16
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:19,800
 You see one bend right here, and then another right here.

17
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:25,280
 And that allows the working end, which we'll talk about in a second, to be aligned with

18
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:29,480
 the long axis of the handle.

19
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:34,600
 And so the working end is represented by the letter A, and it should lie along the long

20
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:39,320
 axis of the instrument for the best balance and control.

21
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:44,560
 So you can see and appreciate how that lines up pretty well with the long axis of the rest

22
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:45,800
 of the instrument there.

23
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:54,680
 And the working end either consists of a blade and a cutting edge, or a nib and a face.

24
00:01:54,680 --> 00:02:01,340
 So the blade and cutting edge refers to terms for a cutting instrument, nib and face for

25
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:03,120
 a non-cutting instrument.

26
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:10,200
 So if we look at these diagrams up here, the nib refers to the entire hook part of this

27
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:14,240
 dental explorer, which is a non-cutting instrument.

28
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:22,880
 The face refers to the little point or the explorer time at the very end of the instrument.

29
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:28,240
 That's the part that's contacting the tooth or the restorative material.

30
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:31,160
 For cutting instruments, we have different terminology.

31
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:36,960
 The blade is the actual cutting part of the instrument, so that's this entire portion

32
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:44,840
 right here, and even more specifically, the cutting edge is the tiny little edge, the

33
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:51,120
 specific sharp part that contacts the tooth or the restorative material.

34
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:56,840
 So the handle is what you hold, the shank is for transition, and the working end is what

35
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:00,000
 actually does the work.

36
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,760
 So now we can talk about some specific non-cutting dental hand instruments.

37
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:10,760
 The first we'll talk about the dental mirror, the small angled mirror, allows you to see

38
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:16,080
 areas of the mouth that otherwise be difficult or impossible to see.

39
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:23,560
 For example, the occlusal surfaces of the upper molars, or lingual surfaces of the upper incisors,

40
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:28,440
 would require you to have the patient sitting way back, and you likely have to twist your

41
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:33,680
 neck somehow to get a clear view, but the mirror makes this a lot easier.

42
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:40,500
 We call this ability to look at oral structures using the mirror in direct vision, as opposed

43
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:48,120
 to direct vision, where your eyes can look at the tooth or structure of interest directly.

44
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:54,360
 The dental explorer provides tactile sensitivity to detect if margins of restorations have

45
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:58,600
 good integrity with the surrounding tooth structure, if the margins of crowns fit well

46
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:03,760
 to the tooth, and there are many other uses, historically they've been used to detect

47
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:11,280
 caries, but doing so to roughly can actually transition an incipient lesion to a cavitated

48
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:16,240
 lesion, like we talked about in the previous two videos.

49
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:21,240
 So we have different variations of the explorer, the shepherd's hook, maybe the most commonly

50
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:28,520
 used, also called a number 23 explorer, this is the classic curved end, again, most commonly

51
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:30,120
 used today.

52
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:36,600
 The back action explorer, or the number 17, is this flatter shorter hook, which is great

53
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:43,920
 for detecting interproximal areas, if you're detecting margins of restorations between

54
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:48,360
 teeth, and also for detecting sub-gingival calculus.

55
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:55,000
 Lastly, there is the number 2 or pigtail explorer, which curves under and around, and can be

56
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,440
 preferred by some providers for detecting crown and restoration margins.

57
00:04:59,440 --> 00:05:05,080
 Again, there are more out there, but these are among the most common and commonly tested.

58
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:10,040
 The peridontal probe is used to routinely measure pocket depths, and they can be used

59
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:14,760
 to measure other things like the width of teeth, the amount of overbite of a patient,

60
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:15,760
 etc.

61
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:22,960
 There are different versions of this as well, the UNC-15 probe measures up to 15 millimeters.

62
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:28,600
 Each mark here, it's kind of small to see, but each mark represents 1 millimeter, and

63
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:37,600
 the bigger hash marks here represent 4-5 millimeters, 9-10, and the final increment 14-15.

64
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:47,360
 The Williams probe has little marks at 1-2-3, it skips 4, goes to 5, skips 6, and then 7-8-9-10,

65
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:54,600
 and the marquee probe shows 3-millimeter increments, 0-3 is this first silver part, then 3-6 is

66
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:59,440
 this thick hash, 6-9-9-12, etc.

67
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:06,680
 The amalgam condenser is used to condense amalgam into the depths and corners of a cavity preparation

68
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:10,320
 to ensure that there are no voids in the material.

69
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:15,240
 It can be also used for condensing composite in much the same way.

70
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:20,840
 Remember, we're still talking about non-cutting instruments here, so this part, the working

71
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:25,960
 end of this instrument is going to be called the nib, and it's the portion of the condensing

72
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:32,360
 instrument that does the work, and the face is the actual end of the instrument that comes

73
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:36,400
 into contact with the restorative material being condensed.

74
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:42,080
 This part is usually smooth, but it can also be serrated.

75
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:48,200
 The ball burnisher is one of my personal favorites, it typically has two ends, a small, round

76
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:53,720
 end, and a larger football-shaped end, and it's really great for burnishing and even

77
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:58,920
 carving the surface of an amalgam restoration before it completely sets to give it some

78
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:04,840
 contour and anatomy, or it can be used in a similar way to smooth out the occlusal surface

79
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:08,120
 of a composite restoration before it's cured.

80
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:13,900
 All right, so that's a brief overview of some of the most common non-cutting instruments.

81
00:07:13,900 --> 00:07:19,440
 Let's talk about some cutting dental hand instruments this time, and we'll look at most

82
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:25,080
 of the, in fact, we'll look at all of the instruments listed here on this slide.

83
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:30,200
 It's a bit of an overgeneralization here, but this is generally what each of these categories

84
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:36,160
 of instruments applies to and works on, and I find it as a helpful memory tool for the

85
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:39,280
 board exam, just to keep this in the back of your head.

86
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:46,440
 So scalars are typically working to remove calculus from teeth, excavators are typically

87
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:53,000
 used on removing carious dentin, or at least working on dentin to structure, chisels for

88
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:00,720
 removing unsupported and/or friable enamel, and the other category, typically these are

89
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:06,520
 going to be used for modifying the restoration of some sort, others basically a category I

90
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:12,720
 threw together of a couple instruments that we'll mention at the end of this section of

91
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:13,840
 the video.

92
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:20,960
 All right, we can now introduce a formula that describes the dimensions of the working

93
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:23,880
 end of most cutting instruments.

94
00:08:23,880 --> 00:08:29,360
 I see this come up on the board exams quite frequently, so let's spend some time unpacking

95
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:33,080
 and really understanding this concept.

96
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:37,720
 So the formula consists of four numbers.

97
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:45,040
 The first number corresponds to the width of the blade in tenths of a millimeter.

98
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:52,400
 So a number 10, a number of 10 means that the blade is one millimeter wide.

99
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:56,960
 So if we're looking at the width of the blade, we're talking about this dimension right in

100
00:08:56,960 --> 00:08:58,960
 here.

101
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:05,120
 So again, a number of 10 for this number refers to one millimeter width of the blade, which

102
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:10,520
 is quite common, and also is the case for the instrument pictured here.

103
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:15,640
 So we'll write 10 down here for the first number.

104
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:26,800
 The second number refers to the angle of the cutting edge to the long axis of the blade.

105
00:09:26,800 --> 00:09:31,800
 So this one is a little bit more involved, the cutting edge is here, the actual part that's

106
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:35,440
 contacting the tooth or restorative material.

107
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:38,880
 And the long axis of the blade runs up here.

108
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:45,120
 I apologize for it, not a perfect straight line there, but you can see what I'm going

109
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:46,520
 for there.

110
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:53,520
 So the angle between these two lines is a perfect 90 degrees in this case.

111
00:09:53,520 --> 00:10:00,000
 So for this, we would just write 90 for our second number.

112
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:06,160
 That's the angle between the blade and the cutting edge.

113
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:13,360
 But if and only if this is 90 degrees, we can actually omit the second number from the

114
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:14,360
 formula.

115
00:10:14,360 --> 00:10:18,040
 And that's because 90 is the most common.

116
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:23,080
 Most of these instruments are going to have a blade that is perpendicular to its cutting

117
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:24,440
 edge.

118
00:10:24,440 --> 00:10:31,280
 So we can omit that number and we just assume that that angle is 90 degrees.

119
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:34,880
 Otherwise we have to list what that angle is.

120
00:10:34,880 --> 00:10:38,080
 All right, let's move to the third number.

121
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:42,560
 This simply corresponds to the length of the blade.

122
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:48,120
 So that's going to be this dimension right in here.

123
00:10:48,120 --> 00:10:53,680
 And so it's simply listing the length of the blade in millimeters.

124
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:54,880
 So this one's nice and easy.

125
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:57,480
 The blade happens to be seven millimeters long.

126
00:10:57,480 --> 00:11:02,200
 So the third number, we just write seven.

127
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:06,960
 And finally, the fourth number, we get a little bit more complicated again.

128
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:13,520
 And the fourth number corresponds to the angle of the blade relative to the long axis of

129
00:11:13,520 --> 00:11:15,240
 the handle.

130
00:11:15,240 --> 00:11:20,360
 So remember the second number was the angle of the cutting edge to the long axis of the

131
00:11:20,360 --> 00:11:21,360
 blade.

132
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:29,360
 Now this is the angle of the blade to the long axis of the handle or the entire instrument.

133
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:34,800
 So because there's a lot more variation in this number, it can literally be anywhere between

134
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:37,480
 zero and 360 degrees.

135
00:11:37,480 --> 00:11:46,120
 So this one is reported not as an angle, but as a percentage of 360 degrees.

136
00:11:46,120 --> 00:11:54,120
 So if this number were 10, let's say, that would signify 10% of 360 degrees, which means

137
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:59,200
 the blade would be 36 degrees off axis.

138
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:08,280
 This instrument's fourth number happens to be 14, which would correspond to 14% of 360

139
00:12:08,280 --> 00:12:11,040
 degrees, which is about 50 degrees.

140
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:16,680
 So the angle that this blade takes relative to the long axis of the handle, which runs

141
00:12:16,680 --> 00:12:22,840
 this way, this angle right in here happens to be 50 degrees.

142
00:12:22,840 --> 00:12:30,600
 So we don't write 50, we write 14, which represents 14% of 360 degrees.

143
00:12:30,600 --> 00:12:35,680
 So if you can remember what each of these numbers just kind of generally represents,

144
00:12:35,680 --> 00:12:38,840
 that will be enormously helpful on the board exam.

145
00:12:38,840 --> 00:12:43,440
 And the idea behind all of this is that if you know the formula of a cutting instrument,

146
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:48,400
 you can visualize and identify what kind of hand instrument it's describing.

147
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:53,520
 And the board exam will sometimes even refer to the specific formulas.

148
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:59,440
 So I'll include them in the slides for each of the following instruments we discuss.

149
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:05,280
 But if you've got nothing else out of this slide, just remember the kind of this part

150
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:10,200
 of the descriptions, and that will be enough to net you at least one or two questions when

151
00:13:10,200 --> 00:13:15,640
 they ask about what a specific number of the formula refers to.

152
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:22,720
 And here's just another way of looking at it, we just broke this down a bit more simply.

153
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:26,360
 So let's start with the dental scalars and we'll actually start with an exception.

154
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:31,520
 The scalars that are used to remove calculus are one of the few cutting instruments that

155
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:34,560
 don't have that four digit formula.

156
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:40,320
 In fact, they follow a different numbering system entirely that we discuss more in depth

157
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:44,720
 in the periodontic series if you're interested in that.

158
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:54,040
 So scalars usually are two-sided and each working end has two cutting edges to it.

159
00:13:54,040 --> 00:13:59,720
 And they adapt closely to the two surface and remove calculus from the crown or the

160
00:13:59,720 --> 00:14:01,160
 root surface.

161
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:07,240
 There are universal types that can be used anywhere in the mouth and then specific types

162
00:14:07,240 --> 00:14:14,560
 like Gracie instruments that are designed for specific teeth and areas in the mouth.

163
00:14:14,560 --> 00:14:18,440
 On here we have another categorization.

164
00:14:18,440 --> 00:14:25,160
 Sickle scalars are scalars that have sharp points at the end of their blades.

165
00:14:25,160 --> 00:14:31,800
 And they are used for calculus above the gum line or super-gingival calculus so that you

166
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:38,160
 don't accidentally shred the gum tissue if you drive that sharp point subgingibly.

167
00:14:38,160 --> 00:14:44,640
 Turettes on the other hand, not pictured here, have rounded edges and are used for calculus

168
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:52,000
 below the gum line because you have less risk of damaging the soft tissue.

169
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:57,760
 Spoon excavators are used for gentle and controlled carries removal.

170
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:05,080
 Their formula is 11.5/7/14 and now you know what all that is talking about.

171
00:15:05,080 --> 00:15:11,080
 The second number is omitted because the cutting edge is perpendicular to the blade as it is

172
00:15:11,080 --> 00:15:13,200
 in most instruments.

173
00:15:13,200 --> 00:15:19,100
 Some operators like using small controlled movements with the spoon excavator to scrape

174
00:15:19,100 --> 00:15:25,720
 carious dentin with the spoon shaped paddles of this excavator.

175
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:32,640
 And this is most useful for very soft, infected dentin removal.

176
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:38,180
 The black spoon is just a larger, more robust version of the spoon excavator.

177
00:15:38,180 --> 00:15:44,720
 It can be also used to burnish metal like a Tafelmeyer matrix band or the margin of a

178
00:15:44,720 --> 00:15:54,280
 gold crown with the rounded back of this black spoon working end.

179
00:15:54,280 --> 00:16:03,580
 You can notice how this first number is larger, 15 referring to the blade width being 1.5mm

180
00:16:03,580 --> 00:16:09,200
 as opposed to a little bit less in the spoon excavator.

181
00:16:09,200 --> 00:16:16,160
 The enamel hatchet is a double ended cutting instrument used for planing walls of enamel.

182
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:23,160
 And the bevel on one end of the instrument planes the facial wall and the bevel on the

183
00:16:23,160 --> 00:16:27,600
 other end of the instrument planes the lingual wall.

184
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:33,800
 Here's a close up view of what that process might look like in part of the cavity preparation

185
00:16:33,800 --> 00:16:35,840
 process.

186
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:40,800
 The bin angle chisel is also a double ended cutting instrument used for planing walls

187
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:41,800
 of enamel.

188
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:47,160
 It's called bin angle because there are two angles in the shank.

189
00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:54,400
 We can locate one here and one here and that's to allow for the proper orientation of the

190
00:16:54,400 --> 00:16:59,520
 blade for optimal control and balance when using this instrument.

191
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:05,800
 It's similar to the enamel hatchet and function but this blade happens to be perpendicular

192
00:17:05,800 --> 00:17:09,240
 to the blade of the enamel hatchet.

193
00:17:09,240 --> 00:17:16,280
 So it enables use in the other side of the mouth or in areas that an enamel hatchet simply

194
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:18,600
 cannot access.

195
00:17:18,600 --> 00:17:24,040
 So if we compare this directly with the enamel hatchet you can see how this blade you can

196
00:17:24,040 --> 00:17:31,400
 kind of see the face of that blade whereas this one is parallel to the screen or perpendicular

197
00:17:31,400 --> 00:17:37,080
 to the screen I should say so that you can notice that those two blades are perpendicular

198
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:39,960
 to each other.

199
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:45,560
 The gingival margin trimmer is similar to the enamel hatchet except this instrument is designed

200
00:17:45,560 --> 00:17:51,200
 for planing enamel at the gingival floor of a preparation rather than the facial and

201
00:17:51,200 --> 00:17:55,320
 lingual walls like the two instruments we just talked about.

202
00:17:55,320 --> 00:18:00,080
 And don't worry we'll talk much more about the anatomy of a cavity preparation including

203
00:18:00,080 --> 00:18:07,120
 the external and internal walls in the next video in the series.

204
00:18:07,120 --> 00:18:14,040
 So the cutting edge of this instrument is most importantly not perpendicular to the long

205
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:16,140
 axis of the blade.

206
00:18:16,140 --> 00:18:22,880
 So this is one of the times when there will be a second number in this four digit formula

207
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:29,160
 for the distinct two versions of this gingival marginal trimmer.

208
00:18:29,160 --> 00:18:36,600
 So the distal margin trimmer has a number, a second number that's greater than 90 which

209
00:18:36,600 --> 00:18:42,200
 is for a gingival floor on the distal side of a tooth.

210
00:18:42,200 --> 00:18:49,440
 The mesial gingival margin trimmer has a number less than 90 which is for the gingival floor

211
00:18:49,440 --> 00:18:52,280
 on the mesial side of a tooth.

212
00:18:52,280 --> 00:18:57,960
 So just remember for the distal you're going to see a number greater than 90 for the mesial

213
00:18:57,960 --> 00:19:05,280
 version of this instrument you're going to see a second number less than 90.

214
00:19:05,280 --> 00:19:11,040
 The main difference and benefit of the gingival margin trimmer over the enamel hatchet is

215
00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:18,400
 that angle of the cutting edge which allows for you to properly bevel the gingival floor.

216
00:19:18,400 --> 00:19:24,440
 And again we can talk about more of that in the rest of the series.

217
00:19:24,440 --> 00:19:29,680
 So you can appreciate how this enamel hatchet again that cutting edge is perpendicular to

218
00:19:29,680 --> 00:19:35,360
 the long axis of the blade whereas that is not the case if we were to follow that long

219
00:19:35,360 --> 00:19:41,360
 axis of the blade this cutting edge is not perpendicular and nor is this one and this

220
00:19:41,360 --> 00:19:47,680
 is showing the two different variations of this instrument depending on which side of

221
00:19:47,680 --> 00:19:51,440
